TITLE='Ella Langley and the "Choosin\' Texas" Phenomenon'
EXCERPT='Country singer-songwriter Ella Langley\'s "Choosin\' Texas" began as a straightforward heartbreak song and became the longest-running No. 1 in Billboard Hot 100 history.'

BODY=r'''Country singer-songwriter Ella Langley's "Choosin' Texas" began as a straightforward heartbreak song and became the longest-running No. 1 in Billboard Hot 100 history. That sentence explains the scale of what happened, but not why the song has lasted. It does not sound designed to smooth away the parts that make it country. Steel guitar curls around a two-step pulse, the narrator drinks alone while a cowboy drifts back toward Texas, and the chorus lands with the plainspoken certainty of a line that feels older than the record itself. The audience kept expanding far beyond Nashville anyway. By the time "Choosin' Texas" broke the Hot 100 record, a song built from familiar country language had become the biggest record in America without changing its accent.

The song began in a much stranger place than its polished heartbreak suggests. Langley was on a writing retreat with Miranda Lambert, Luke Dick and Joybeth Taylor when Lambert started telling a story about being pulled over with a pet kangaroo in the car. The detail that stuck was not the animal but the Texas license plate. Langley answered with a phrase that immediately sounded like a song, "She's from Texas, I can tell." From there, the room turned a roadside story into a romantic one. Langley has said the song came together in roughly half an hour. The whole track is built around that one turn, with the title serving as both the conclusion and the hook. By the time the narrator says he is "choosin' Texas," the listener already knows exactly what she has lost.

What makes the writing work is the way geography carries the emotional weight. Tennessee is the life the narrator thought she was building. Texas becomes the place pulling him away. Abilene, Memphis, the Smoky Mountains and I-40 are not there as decorative country references. They make the breakup feel physical, as if the relationship is being measured in roads, rooms and state lines. Even the nod to George Strait's "Amarillo by Morning" works as a warning the narrator only understands in hindsight. The central idea is specific enough to be unmistakably country, but the feeling underneath it is broader. Langley told Rolling Stone that people can relate to wanting something that does not want them back, whether that is a relationship, a job or something else. All the Texas imagery grows from that simple feeling.

There is also a reason the song feels connected to older country writing without sounding like a period piece. Langley grew up in Hope Hull, Alabama, learned old songs around the family piano and started playing cover sets in bars at 18 before moving to Nashville. Her debut album, *Hungover*, introduced a voice that could be funny, conversational and bruised in the same verse. "You Look Like You Love Me," her breakthrough duet with Riley Green, was built around spoken sections that her label initially wanted her to sing. Langley held her ground, and the song's success gave her more room to trust her instincts. On *Dandelion*, that confidence is even clearer. The album opens and closes with "Froggy Went A Courtin'," one of the first songs she remembers singing with her grandfather, then moves into modern country that is comfortable with pop melody while keeping its older roots in view.

Miranda Lambert's role in that evolution goes beyond a famous co-write. She helped write "Choosin' Texas," sings background vocals on the recording and later co-produced *Dandelion* with Langley and Ben West. Their connection makes sense because both artists are drawn to plain language, barroom detail and women who get to tell the story from their own side. The finished record leaves enough space for that writing to carry the song. Steel guitar supplies the ache, the rhythm keeps it moving, and Langley's vocal never oversells the punch line. The arrangement stays recognizably country even as the melody lands fast. It can sit on country radio, a streaming playlist or a pop countdown because the hook is immediate and the country details are doing part of the work.

The official video expands that world instead of simply illustrating the lyrics. Langley co-directed it with Wales Toney and Caylee Robillard at the Stagecoach Ballroom in Fort Worth, turning the song into a compact Western melodrama. *Yellowstone* actor Luke Grimes plays the boyfriend, Ava Phillippe is the woman drawing his attention back toward Texas, and Lambert appears as the singer onstage. Kaitlin Butts turns up as an old friend who can see the trouble before Langley's character fully accepts it. The setting matters as much as the casting. Couples two-step beneath the lights, rodeo figures and Texas musicians fill the room, and the bar feels busy enough to make the jealousy believable. By the end, Langley leaves with Lambert and the band, giving the song's narrator an exit the lyric itself never spells out.

Long before the chart records piled up, "Choosin' Texas" had already proved that it could live onstage. Langley began playing it before the studio version became a phenomenon, and Lambert also worked it into her own shows. The live versions reveal something the polished single can almost disguise. The chorus is built for a room full of people to finish together. At CMA Fest, it arrives as a full-scale country singalong, with the crowd answering the hook as if the song has been around for years. At Billboard Women in Music, Langley performed it in a more stripped-down setting and the writing still carried the room. The song works in a bar, on country radio and on a festival stage with the same basic arrangement. People who first found it through streaming can hear the same song open up into a live anthem.

The chart story eventually became impossible to separate from the song. "Choosin' Texas" has spent 23 weeks at No. 1 on the Hot 100, the longest reign in the chart's history, and it was named Billboard's Song of the Summer. Earlier in the year, Langley became the first woman to lead the Hot 100, Hot Country Songs and Country Airplay at the same time. Then country songs filled the entire Hot 100 top five for the first time, with Langley appearing twice. Together, those milestones show how far the song moved beyond one format. A record built from steel guitar, two-stepping and a love triangle framed by Tennessee and Texas was competing directly with the biggest pop, rap and R&B releases in the country, and week after week listeners kept it there.

Langley did not create that shift by herself. Country had been building a much larger streaming audience for several years, with artists such as Morgan Wallen, Zach Bryan and Luke Combs reaching listeners far beyond the format's old radio boundaries. Shaboozey's "A Bar Song (Tipsy)" turned a country-rap blend into one of the defining pop hits of 2024, while Wallen's "Last Night" had already shown how long a contemporary country single could dominate the Hot 100. Streaming changed the route into the genre as much as it changed the size of the audience. Listeners no longer had to choose a country station before hearing the songs. A track could move from a playlist, a short video or a friend's feed into everyday listening, putting Nashville records in the same queue as pop and hip-hop.

Post Malone shows the other side of the same change. His move into country with *F-1 Trillion* was not treated as a one-song experiment. He worked with Nashville writers and artists, made his Grand Ole Opry debut and built a full album around the genre. "I Had Some Help," his duet with Morgan Wallen, topped both country and pop airplay. That kind of crossover once looked like two separate radio worlds briefly sharing a record. Now it feels much closer to the normal shape of the market. Beyonce's *Cowboy Carter* and Shaboozey's rise opened different conversations about country's boundaries, but Post Malone's Nashville immersion made another point clear. Mainstream stars are moving toward country at the same time country artists are reaching pop-scale audiences on their own terms.

Country's boom is visible in what people wear and do as well as what they stream. Western boots and hats have moved through mainstream fashion, line-dance nights have spread well beyond Nashville, and major festivals have made rodeo and honky-tonk imagery familiar to audiences who may not follow country radio. "Choosin' Texas" fits that moment almost perfectly. The title became a caption, a joke and a shorthand for choosing one thing over another. The video gives viewers a ballroom full of cues they recognize before the plot develops, from the two-stepping to the rodeo crowd to Lambert behind the microphone. The record's cultural reach comes partly from how easily those details travel while still feeling tied to a particular place and style of music.

For Langley, the connection to country's past is more deliberate than the size of the Hot 100 numbers might suggest. *Dandelion* includes her version of Kitty Wells' "It Wasn't God Who Made Honky Tonk Angels," a foundational song by one of the women who changed what a female country voice could say on record. Langley also talks openly about learning from Lambert and growing up on older songs, even as her own audience now stretches far beyond country radio. In that context, "Choosin' Texas" belongs to the same country lineage as the rest of Langley's work, only with an audience large enough to make it a pop record too. She opens the album with a family folk song, puts a Kitty Wells standard in the middle of it and still ends up at the top of the Hot 100.

Strip away the record-book numbers and "Choosin' Texas" is still easy to understand. The song has a memorable title, a chorus that can be learned in one listen, a production that gives the melody room, and a story with enough detail to feel lived in. It also arrived when American listeners were already making country part of their everyday pop listening. Langley did not have to rebuild the song for that audience. The steel guitar, two-step rhythm and Tennessee-versus-Texas heartbreak stayed intact as the crowd around them grew. The Hot 100 record turned that reach into history. The more revealing achievement is that one of America's biggest pop songs still sounds completely at home in a Texas dance hall.'''

def fresh_read():
    return runner.http(runner.DESK_API+"?nocache="+str(time.time_ns()), runner.desk_key())

def main():
    runner.load_env()
    if EXCERPT not in BODY or not BODY.startswith(EXCERPT):
        raise RuntimeError("excerpt must be the literal lead")
    if len(BODY.split()) < 1500:
        raise RuntimeError("longread below text-stage floor")
    if any(x in BODY for x in ("—","’","“","”")):
        raise RuntimeError("non-canon punctuation in body")
    if re.search(r"\[(youtube|apple|photo|tickets|instagram|tiktok):", BODY, re.I):
        raise RuntimeError("text-stage draft must not contain media yet")
    paras=[p.strip() for p in BODY.split("\n\n") if p.strip()]
    sizes=[len(p.split()) for p in paras]
    if any(n < 80 or n > 200 for n in sizes):
        raise RuntimeError("paragraph outside 80-200 word text-stage band: "+repr(sizes))
    if any(len(re.split(r"(?<=[.!?])\s+", p)) < 2 for p in paras):
        raise RuntimeError("one-sentence paragraph")
    before=fresh_read()
    existing=next((p for p in before["posts"] if str(p.get("id"))==POST_ID),None)
    if existing and existing.get("status")!="draft":
        raise RuntimeError("refusing to replace non-draft Ella longread")
    old_cover=copy.deepcopy((existing or {}).get("cover")) if existing else None
    rec={
        "id":POST_ID,
        "type":"news",
        "tag":"Feature",
        "artist":"Ella Langley",
        "title":TITLE,
        "excerpt":EXCERPT,
        "body":BODY,
        "date":"2026-10-01",
        "status":"draft",
        "pinned":False,
        "cover":old_cover or {"kind":"preset","i":0},
    }
    def mutate(posts):
        p=next((x for x in posts if str(x.get("id"))==POST_ID),None)
        if p is None:
            posts.insert(0,copy.deepcopy(rec))
            return posts[0]
        p.clear()
        p.update(copy.deepcopy(rec))
        return p
    runner.guarded_write(mutate)
    for attempt in range(20):
        now=next((p for p in fresh_read()["posts"] if str(p.get("id"))==POST_ID),None)
        if now and now.get("body")==BODY and now.get("status")=="draft":
            print("ELLA_LONGREAD_TEXT_DRAFT_OK",len(BODY.split()),len(paras),sizes)
            print("MEDIA_STAGE","pending")
            return
        time.sleep(2)
    raise RuntimeError("Ella text draft did not propagate")

if __name__=="__main__":
    main()
